// Mountain snow and valley glaciers (geography geoMtn*), where the height data apply and the ground
// is high (relief .3: 2.1 km real): faces toward the pole hold snow up to 350 m lower and faces toward
// the equator as much higher (from 6° of slope, past 6° of latitude), and the ground's height against
// its surroundings (the detail tile's real height against the 10 km cube) lowers the line in hollows
// (up to 350 m) and raises it on ridges (up to 250 m, blown bare); gentle hollows 100–500 m deep
// can carry a glacier.
